  "duration": 55.915,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.508,
      "text": "One of the universe's largest black holes has mysteriously vanished.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lonely Black Hole Relic Shines Light on Young Universe (gemini1603b).tiff",
        "credit": "Simulation Credit: NASA, ESA, and D. Coe, J. Anderson, and R. van der Marel (Space Telescope Science Institute) Acknowledgment for Omega Centauri Image: NASA, ESA, and the Hubble SM4 ERO Team Science ",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lonely_Black_Hole_Relic_Shines_Light_on_Young_Universe_(gemini1603b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.5,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.508,
      "end": 14.103,
      "text": "At the center of galaxy A2261-BCG, astronomers expected a monster up to 100 billion times the mass of our Sun.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Galaxy Cluster Abell 2261.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, HST",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galaxy_Cluster_Abell_2261.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.5,
          "end": 11.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 11.133,
          "end": 14.1,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 14.103,
      "end": 22.873,
      "text": "Instead, Hubble observations showed a bloated core measuring 10,000 light-years across with no concentrated peak of starlight.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 14.1,
          "end": 22.867,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 22.873,
      "end": 32.893,
      "text": "Deep Chandra X-ray searches from 1999 and 2004 detected no signs of superheated material falling into a central black hole.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 22.867,
          "end": 32.9,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.893,
      "end": 41.238,
      "text": "In 2018, a team led by Kayhan Gultekin of the University of Michigan tried again but also failed to find anything.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "AngellHall2010.JPG",
        "credit": "AndrewHorne",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AngellHall2010.JPG"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 32.9,
          "end": 37.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 37.067,
          "end": 41.233,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 41.238,
      "end": 49.783,
      "text": "The leading theory is that asymmetric gravitational waves from a giant cosmic merger kicked the black hole completely out of the galaxy.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "When Black Holes Collide.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Blueshift",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:When_Black_Holes_Collide.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 41.233,
          "end": 49.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 49.783,
      "end": 55.565,
      "text": "Today, astronomers are still searching deep space to find this invisible, runaway giant.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lonely Black Hole Relic Shines Light on Young Universe (gemini1603b).tiff",
        "credit": "Simulation Credit: NASA, ESA, and D. Coe, J. Anderson, and R. van der Marel (Space Telescope Science Institute) Acknowledgment for Omega Centauri Image: NASA, ESA, and the Hubble SM4 ERO Team Science ",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lonely_Black_Hole_Relic_Shines_Light_on_Young_Universe_(gemini1603b).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 49.767,
          "end": 55.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
